# GLib, GObject and libvips allocate tables at startup and keep them for the
# life of the process on purpose. Those are reachable, not lost, but the leak
# checker cannot tell the difference from outside, so name them here.
#
# A pattern matches anywhere in the stack, so the allocator entries below —
# g_malloc and friends — also hide any leak of this binding's own that was
# allocated through GLib. That is the price of running against GLib at all:
# what this check can genuinely find is a leak on a plain malloc/strdup path,
# which is what the C core mostly uses. Leaks of libvips-allocated memory are
# covered by internal/soak against libvips' own counters instead.

leak:g_type_register
leak:g_type_class_ref
leak:g_quark
leak:g_once_init_enter
leak:g_malloc
leak:g_realloc
leak:g_slice_alloc
leak:vips_init
leak:vips_operation_new
leak:vips_cache_operation_add
leak:vips__thread
leak:dlopen
